I disagree here. As another poster has mentioned, some people actually avoid 
advancing attack spells because there are disadvantages in how targetted 
spells are implemented. Advancing a skill should not be disadvantageous to 
the player. Fireballs and cones of cold should allow for a directional input 
(producing a ray effect) as well as a specific square to "aim" at.

>    I think the fireball effect is somewhat weird, but I would rationalize
> it this way: "Skilled" doesn't actually mean you're more skilled.  It just 
> means you're a more powerful spell caster.  Which you ought to remember, 
> and not cast your big powerful fireball spells at close range anymore.

That makes no sense at all. First of all, you become a "more powerful spell 
caster" simply by levelling up; you gain skill levels by practicing 
particular types of spells. And in this case, gaining a skill level causes 
you to *lose* the ability to fire a spell in a particular direction; you 
*have* to be able to see a target point within your range.

>    (I think if the DevTeam meant the fireball effect to be different, 
> they'd have changed it by now.  

By that line of reasoning, there will never be a 3.5.0.

